gst_mini_object_is_writable(): lock-free fast path for parentless objects

Upstream gst_mini_object_is_writable() unconditionally acquires the
priv_uint spinlock (a CAS to LOCKED plus an atomic store to release) just
to discover that the object has no parents -- which is the case for every
event, caps and query, and for buffers themselves (a buffer's memories
have parents; the buffer does not). This patch returns early on a single
atomic load when the state is PRIV_DATA_STATE_NO_PARENT.

Safety: mutating the parent list requires holding a reference to the
object, which contradicts the sole ownership (refcount==1 / single
exclusive lock) already established before the fast path runs. The
answer is exactly as current as the locked path's, which also drops its
lock before returning.

Besides the cycles, this makes the common-case query genuinely read-only:
the spinlock CAS dirtied the object's cache line on every check, so
concurrent writability checks on a shared object bounced the line
between cores.

Measured (debugoptimized, x86-64, pinned, 100M iters):
  is_writable(buffer):          17.3 ns -> 2.2 ns   (7.9x)
  is_writable(event):           19.4 ns -> 2.3 ns   (8.3x)
  make_writable(when writable): 15.8 ns -> 3.5 ns   (4.5x)

Call frequency (LD_PRELOAD counter, cross-DSO calls only, so a lower
bound): 1 is_writable per buffer per in-place transform (volume:
10000/10000 buffers); ~1.7 make_writable per compressed packet in Ogg
Opus decode (39548 over one file); bursts during caps negotiation.

Core test suites pass (gstbuffer, gstpad, gstevent, gstmemory).
Not upstreamed yet: candidate follow-up after the gst_pad_forward MR.

diff --git a/subprojects/gstreamer/gst/gstminiobject.c b/subprojects/gstreamer/gst/gstminiobject.c
index bf0a9fcbc9..8622d90fbe 100644
--- a/subprojects/gstreamer/gst/gstminiobject.c
+++ b/subprojects/gstreamer/gst/gstminiobject.c
@@ -373,6 +373,16 @@ gst_mini_object_is_writable (const GstMiniObject * mini_object)
   if (!result)
     return result;
 
+  /* Fast path: no parents and no qdata, the common case for events, caps,
+   * queries and most buffers. A single atomic read suffices: mutating the
+   * parent list requires holding a reference to the object, which
+   * contradicts the sole ownership established above, and the answer is
+   * exactly as current as the locked path's, which also drops its lock
+   * before returning. */
+  if (G_LIKELY (g_atomic_int_get ((gint *) & mini_object->priv_uint) ==
+          PRIV_DATA_STATE_NO_PARENT))
+    return TRUE;
+
   /* We are writable ourselves, but are there parents and are they all
    * writable too? */
   priv_state = lock_priv_pointer (GST_MINI_OBJECT_CAST (mini_object));
